export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Sometimes, affordances may not be enough to guide players. That is when you should use <em>signifiers</em>—additional visual cues that inform players of certain functions. To make a distinction: an affordance is a built-in component of an object; <strong>a </strong><em><strong>signifier</strong></em>, on the other hand, is <strong>a label explaining the usage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Going back to the door example, the flat metal plate is an affordance, but the word PUSH is a signifier.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/88fada83eeccfead2866.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/88fada83eeccfead2866.webp\" width=\"561\" height=\"473\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/f796d557a3748f0b6f3d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/f796d557a3748f0b6f3d.webp\" width=\"954\" height=\"472\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/521db863094977107295.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/521db863094977107295.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Many designers use <em>affordances</em> and <em>signifiers</em> interchangeably; they just want to convey whether an object is interactive or not. But we think it helps to distinguish them, so you can identify where the issues lie in your props.</p>\n<p>In the top picture, the white stripes signify that your character can stand on the ledge. When there are no stripes, he can only grab and hang (bottom picture).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In the top picture, the tree branches are wrapped with ropes, signifying to players that they can use his rope equipment there.</p>\n<p><sub>Note: the (L1) button prompt is a UI element, not a signifier.</sub></p>\n<p>In the bottom picture, you can see some tire tracks on the rock surface, signifying to players that they can drive on it. The angle of the rock and the tracks also subtly lead players to the next section in the level.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/eb8a31f6a00fedf0c359.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/eb8a31f6a00fedf0c359.webp\" width=\"680\" height=\"406\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/39bdd7161f5aa4109334.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/39bdd7161f5aa4109334.webp\" width=\"1095\" height=\"613\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d828325bf8a1281dd338.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d828325bf8a1281dd338.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/68cbed1ed79523821137.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/68cbed1ed79523821137.webp\" width=\"609\" height=\"347\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Signifiers can be a text, a symbol, or an object as illustrated.</p>\n<p>In the top picture, the texture signifies to players that they can run on these walls.</p>\n<p>In the bottom picture, the damage on the wooden boards signifies they are breakable.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Similar to affordances, you can add signifiers in your block-out early on to provide an outline to your level. In this example, the EVACUATION sign was added in the block-out during the blocking stage.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/4c316fe864f6ff4fca8b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/4c316fe864f6ff4fca8b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d4c8b378cabacdb0963f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d4c8b378cabacdb0963f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"630\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
